WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:06.074
This is Part 1 of the technical deep dive
for the Federal Modernization Workbook's

3
00:00:06.074 --> 00:00:09.746
companion guide, Attribute-Driven
Migration to Entra ID,

4
00:00:09.746 --> 00:00:13.574
for the engineers, administrators
and I S S O's who will run it.

5
00:00:13.574 --> 00:00:15.037
The idea is simple.

6
00:00:15.037 --> 00:00:19.812
In Active Directory, where an object sat
in the tree decided what applied to it.

7
00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:23.624
In Entra ID, what the object
says about itself decides.

8
00:00:23.624 --> 00:00:25.700
The deep dive is split by track.

9
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.025
This part is the model and
the HR track, the people.

10
00:00:29.025 --> 00:00:33.250
Part 2 is devices, servers
and non-person identities.

11
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.860
Let's start with why.

12
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:39.997
Here's why this matters.

13
00:00:39.997 --> 00:00:42.431
The organizational units, or O U's,

14
00:00:42.431 --> 00:00:46.485
of an Active Directory forest
were never just folders.

15
00:00:46.485 --> 00:00:49.243
That one tree decided who
could manage an object,

16
00:00:49.243 --> 00:00:53.173
which policy reached it, which office
owned it and what it could touch.

17
00:00:53.173 --> 00:00:54.998
Entra ID has no tree.

18
00:00:54.998 --> 00:00:56.661
Its directory is flat.

19
00:00:56.661 --> 00:00:59.004
So all four of those
jobs have to move into

20
00:00:59.004 --> 00:01:01.623
attributes and the
mechanisms that read them.

21
00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:04.959
One HR-sourced stamp
drives group membership,

22
00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:08.647
delegation, policy, licensing
and drift detection.

23
00:01:08.647 --> 00:01:11.984
If the stamp is wrong, all
five are wrong at once.

24
00:01:11.984 --> 00:01:14.760
So, Module 1, the model.

25
00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.908
Module 1 is the model: what
the O U tree was really doing,

26
00:01:21.908 --> 00:01:24.379
the four tracks its identities move on,

27
00:01:24.379 --> 00:01:29.090
what the migration accomplishes and
the rules behind it, a short primer on

28
00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:34.480
attributes, and the nine stages that get
you there, each with a decider and a gate.

29
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.118
This slide is the translation table,
and it's the heart of the model.

30
00:01:42.118 --> 00:01:44.168
Placement becomes attributes.

31
00:01:44.168 --> 00:01:47.254
Groups built from O U's
become dynamic groups.

32
00:01:47.254 --> 00:01:49.967
Delegation moves to administrative units.

33
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:54.680
And Group Policy inheritance becomes
Conditional Access and Intune targeting.

34
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.814
One rule on the right matters more
than it looks: rules read codes,

35
00:01:58.814 --> 00:02:03.805
never display names, because a display
name breaks the day someone retypes it.

36
00:02:03.805 --> 00:02:06.808
And some native fields can't
be read by rules at all,

37
00:02:06.808 --> 00:02:09.518
which is why the schema
carries coded copies.

38
00:02:09.518 --> 00:02:13.860
But the tree held more than
people, so next, the four tracks.

39
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.869
This slide is the map for
both parts of the deep dive,

40
00:02:20.869 --> 00:02:24.360
because one O U tree holds
four different populations.

41
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.597
People are the HR track: HR and the
contractor source write their attributes.

42
00:02:29.597 --> 00:02:34.459
End-user devices are the Intune track:
device provisioning writes theirs.

43
00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:39.360
Servers are the Arc track: Arc
onboarding and Azure Policy write tags.

44
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.069
And service accounts, service
principals, workload identities and AI

45
00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:48.860
agents are the non-person track: their
owner's record is the source, not HR.

46
00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.620
So what's the end state?

47
00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.383
Here's what the whole migration is for.

48
00:02:56.383 --> 00:03:01.973
Every person, device, server and workload
is governed by attributes that are true,

49
00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:05.144
owned by exactly one
source, and read by policy.

50
00:03:05.144 --> 00:03:10.077
Joining, moving and leaving change
access with no ticket, every non-person

51
00:03:10.077 --> 00:03:15.170
identity has an owner and a managed
credential, and AD can finally be retired.

52
00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.918
The table is how you know you're there,
track by track: every account traced to HR,

53
00:03:19.918 --> 00:03:23.331
every device and server
targeted by its own attributes,

54
00:03:23.331 --> 00:03:26.595
every service account owned,
and a clean drift report.

55
00:03:26.595 --> 00:03:29.441
That's design guidance
on the books' own gates,

56
00:03:29.441 --> 00:03:33.132
and the last column names the
federal rule each one serves.

57
00:03:33.132 --> 00:03:34.720
So, which rules?

58
00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:42.621
Each track supports federal rules
the agency already has to meet,

59
00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:44.158
in the program's mapping.

60
00:03:44.158 --> 00:03:50.034
The HR track carries account management,
personnel termination and transfer,

61
00:03:50.034 --> 00:03:56.995
and identifier management in NIST 800-53,
plus M-19-17 and the Federal HR 2.0 memo.

62
00:03:56.995 --> 00:04:02.145
PIV sign-in serves M-22-09 and FIPS 201.

63
00:04:02.145 --> 00:04:06.659
Intune and Arc carry the
configuration and inventory controls,

64
00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:11.684
the non-person track carries privileged
accounts and authenticators,

65
00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:14.920
and every track feeds
monitoring under M-26-14.

66
00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:19.508
Your authorizing official decides; the
architecture supplies the evidence.

67
00:04:19.508 --> 00:04:22.160
Next, a primer on attributes.

68
00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:30.522
Before the stages, a short primer,
because every later slide leans on it.

69
00:04:30.522 --> 00:04:33.897
In AD, where an object
sits does half the work.

70
00:04:33.897 --> 00:04:38.134
In Entra ID, what the object says
about itself has to do all of it.

71
00:04:38.134 --> 00:04:42.760
The directory is flat, the schema is
fixed, and you reach it through Graph.

72
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.485
Write control moves to roles
scoped by administrative units.

73
00:04:46.485 --> 00:04:51.772
And the row that shapes everything
is the last one: for synced users,

74
00:04:51.772 --> 00:04:57.348
AD still masters most attributes, so
edit them in AD or HR, not in Entra ID.

75
00:04:57.348 --> 00:04:59.620
Now the attributes themselves.

76
00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:07.607
These are the attributes your team
already knows, and what each becomes.

77
00:05:07.607 --> 00:05:10.958
distinguishedName comes across,
but never use it for policy,

78
00:05:10.958 --> 00:05:13.169
because it's the placement we're leaving.

79
00:05:13.169 --> 00:05:15.763
Rules can't read manager or employeeType,

80
00:05:15.763 --> 00:05:18.508
which is why the program
keeps coded copies.

81
00:05:18.508 --> 00:05:23.483
The disabled bit becomes accountEnabled,
and every access rule should require it.

82
00:05:23.483 --> 00:05:28.126
And two have no attribute at all:
Kerberos service principal names stay

83
00:05:28.126 --> 00:05:32.770
in AD until the workloads move, and
admin count gives way to roles and PIM.

84
00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.750
So where do the values live?

85
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:41.688
This is the store table, and
both parts point back to it.

86
00:05:41.688 --> 00:05:45.887
There are eight places an attribute can
live, in the order to reach for them.

87
00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:47.563
Native properties first.

88
00:05:47.563 --> 00:05:53.175
Then extension attributes one to fifteen,
which rules can read but anyone can see.

89
00:05:53.175 --> 00:05:55.938
Directory extensions when
you need more slots.

90
00:05:55.938 --> 00:06:00.738
Custom security attributes for sensitive
values that dynamic rules can't read.

91
00:06:00.738 --> 00:06:06.751
Then device properties, Azure tags, and
certificate user I Ds, the PIV binding.

92
00:06:06.751 --> 00:06:08.788
Pick the store by who has to read it.

93
00:06:08.788 --> 00:06:10.310
Now, the path.

94
00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:18.448
Here's the whole path on one page,
laid out this way for accountability.

95
00:06:18.448 --> 00:06:21.986
Nine stages, from reading
the tree to correcting drift.

96
00:06:21.986 --> 00:06:25.813
Every stage has exactly one
decider, taken from the books'

97
00:06:25.813 --> 00:06:29.722
two responsibility matrices,
and a gate that says it's done.

98
00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:34.316
Identity engineering owns most of
the plumbing, application owners own

99
00:06:34.316 --> 00:06:38.985
their access rules, and the CISO owns
the controls and the drift findings.

100
00:06:38.985 --> 00:06:40.539
And the gates are the point:

101
00:06:40.539 --> 00:06:43.860
nothing grants production
access until it's been proved.

102
00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.820
Stage zero first.

103
00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:53.340
Stage zero matters because the
target has to reproduce intent,

104
00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.820
not structure, and you can't
reproduce what you haven't read.

105
00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.558
It's entirely read-only.

106
00:06:58.558 --> 00:07:01.765
Capture each O U's placement,
its Group Policy links,

107
00:07:01.765 --> 00:07:05.507
and especially the permissions
someone delegated by hand.

108
00:07:05.507 --> 00:07:10.202
Then decide every group's fate, and
split the Group Policy settings by track:

109
00:07:10.202 --> 00:07:14.820
user settings go with people, computer
settings with devices and servers.

110
00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.363
Two traps: a policy linked at a site
looks unlinked if you only search O U's,

111
00:07:19.363 --> 00:07:22.982
and the protected-account
marker overstates privilege.

112
00:07:22.982 --> 00:07:24.740
Now the attributes.

113
00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:33.578
Module 2 is the HR track: sourcing
and proving the attributes of people.

114
00:07:33.578 --> 00:07:37.456
Every mechanism later in this
session reads these values,

115
00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:40.365
so this is where the
migration is won or lost.

116
00:07:40.365 --> 00:07:45.315
We'll cover who owns each fact,
Federal HR 2.0 as the coming source,

117
00:07:45.315 --> 00:07:49.599
how HR data reaches Entra ID,
the facts HR doesn't supply,

118
00:07:49.599 --> 00:07:54.350
the schema and its single
writer, and then proof and sync.

119
00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:01.513
This is the most important
ownership decision in the program.

120
00:08:01.513 --> 00:08:04.525
Each fact has exactly
one authoritative system.

121
00:08:04.525 --> 00:08:07.475
HR owns who a worker
is and where they sit.

122
00:08:07.475 --> 00:08:10.160
The identity platform
owns how they prove it,

123
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.700
and whether a request
is allowed right now.

124
00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.488
The card system owns the PIV credential.

125
00:08:15.488 --> 00:08:17.183
Everything else is a copy,

126
00:08:17.183 --> 00:08:21.138
and corrections flow back to
the source, never into the copy.

127
00:08:21.138 --> 00:08:23.512
The other decision is the person key.

128
00:08:23.512 --> 00:08:28.620
Make it a key the agency controls, with
the HR system's own number mapped to it,

129
00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:32.162
so changing HR systems
doesn't break every identity.

130
00:08:32.162 --> 00:08:33.890
And that change is coming.

131
00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:41.159
Federal HR 2.0 matters
because it's about to become

132
00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:44.677
the source for most federal
employee attributes.

133
00:08:44.677 --> 00:08:50.715
O M B and O P M are consolidating core
HR onto one government-wide platform.

134
00:08:50.715 --> 00:08:57.428
The contract went to Oracle in June 2026,
and most agencies move in fiscal 2027.

135
00:08:57.428 --> 00:08:59.902
Confirm that schedule
with the program office.

136
00:08:59.902 --> 00:09:02.163
Here's the highpoint: nothing from O P M

137
00:09:02.163 --> 00:09:05.227
specifies how that platform
feeds your directory.

138
00:09:05.227 --> 00:09:07.515
The Entra design is yours to make.

139
00:09:07.515 --> 00:09:10.865
The book's advice is to put the
identity feed in front of the

140
00:09:10.865 --> 00:09:14.920
steering committee early, because
that window closes at go-live.

141
00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.395
So how does the record
actually reach Entra ID?

142
00:09:21.395 --> 00:09:24.718
Oracle's platform has no
native Entra connector,

143
00:09:24.718 --> 00:09:27.957
so the path is A P I-driven
inbound provisioning.

144
00:09:27.957 --> 00:09:31.717
Middleware in your own boundary
validates each HR event,

145
00:09:31.717 --> 00:09:35.396
records its provenance, and
posts it to Microsoft Graph.

146
00:09:35.396 --> 00:09:37.595
Then it lands in one of two places.

147
00:09:37.595 --> 00:09:40.304
For most users today
that's Active Directory,

148
00:09:40.304 --> 00:09:43.233
which syncs up, and the
Entra copies are read-only.

149
00:09:43.233 --> 00:09:46.383
For cloud-managed users
it's Entra ID directly.

150
00:09:46.383 --> 00:09:49.170
The two converge population by population.

151
00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:51.847
It's included with the P1 and P2 licenses,

152
00:09:51.847 --> 00:09:57.150
and it's been generally available in
the government cloud since June 2025.

153
00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.367
HR supplies most of the
person, but not everything,

154
00:10:04.367 --> 00:10:08.088
and every gap needs a named
writer or it becomes drift.

155
00:10:08.088 --> 00:10:13.315
Contractors aren't in the HR system, so
each of their facts gets its own authority,

156
00:10:13.315 --> 00:10:16.087
like the contract record
or the PIV system.

157
00:10:16.087 --> 00:10:20.663
Device attributes come from device
provisioning, never from the user's record.

158
00:10:20.663 --> 00:10:25.274
And where someone signs in from is
a sign-in signal, not an HR fact.

159
00:10:25.274 --> 00:10:28.992
On the right is the design
that survives the HR cutover:

160
00:10:28.992 --> 00:10:34.113
an agency-owned key, standard data
elements, and one door into the directory.

161
00:10:34.113 --> 00:10:37.770
That makes the switch a source
swap rather than a redesign.

162
00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.986
The schema is where names,
codes and slots get fixed,

163
00:10:44.986 --> 00:10:47.132
and every rule will depend on it.

164
00:10:47.132 --> 00:10:51.917
The chain on the left is the ownership
model: the standard defines the code,

165
00:10:51.917 --> 00:10:56.232
HR owns each person's value, and
one provisioning service writes it.

166
00:10:56.232 --> 00:10:59.128
Access targeting uses three coded slots:

167
00:10:59.128 --> 00:11:02.620
organization, worker type
and position category.

168
00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:06.798
Some native fields, like hire
date, are readable only in preview,

169
00:11:06.798 --> 00:11:09.208
so they stay out of production rules.

170
00:11:09.208 --> 00:11:12.833
The panel under the table is the
rule that holds it all together.

171
00:11:12.833 --> 00:11:15.508
The provisioning service
is the only writer.

172
00:11:15.508 --> 00:11:20.820
Remove the help desk and self-service
paths, and treat any other write as drift.

173
00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:28.658
Stage two is the one teams want to
skip, and it's the one you can't.

174
00:11:28.658 --> 00:11:31.692
Everything downstream trusts these values,

175
00:11:31.692 --> 00:11:36.121
so a bad HR record becomes bad
access faster than any ticket queue.

176
00:11:36.121 --> 00:11:39.476
Baseline HR against reality,
fix errors at the source,

177
00:11:39.476 --> 00:11:44.358
and match every enabled account to a
worker record before the first full sync,

178
00:11:44.358 --> 00:11:48.095
because that match is what
prevents duplicate identities.

179
00:11:48.095 --> 00:11:50.562
The threshold is the agency's own call;

180
00:11:50.562 --> 00:11:54.858
ninety-nine percent on the pilot is
a starting proposal, not a rule.

181
00:11:54.858 --> 00:11:58.232
And expect a burst of serious
findings in the first month.

182
00:11:58.232 --> 00:12:01.257
That's the measurement working,
not the program failing.

183
00:12:01.257 --> 00:12:02.810
Then, sync.

184
00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:09.164
Stage three is the plumbing between HR,

185
00:12:09.164 --> 00:12:14.372
Active Directory and Entra ID while they
coexist, and failures here are quiet.

186
00:12:14.372 --> 00:12:18.589
The default sync engine is Cloud Sync
unless you have a Connect-only need,

187
00:12:18.589 --> 00:12:20.697
with password hash sync everywhere.

188
00:12:20.697 --> 00:12:23.606
Then map the dates and
the transitional fields,

189
00:12:23.606 --> 00:12:27.260
and run in shadow mode until
every difference is explained.

190
00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:28.385
Three traps.

191
00:12:28.385 --> 00:12:33.560
A soft or hard match can overwrite cloud
values with stale on-premises ones.

192
00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:38.060
Older Connect Sync servers
stop syncing in April 2027.

193
00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:41.666
And a provisioning record with a
missing field can clear a value,

194
00:12:41.666 --> 00:12:43.435
so always send the whole record.

195
00:12:43.435 --> 00:12:45.450
Now, the mechanisms.

196
00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.900
Module 3 puts the HR
track's attributes to work.

197
00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:55.811
Dynamic user groups
replace the O U subtrees,

198
00:12:55.811 --> 00:13:01.475
administrative units take over delegation,
and Conditional Access targets personas.

199
00:13:01.475 --> 00:13:04.145
Then the dangers of dynamic user groups,

200
00:13:04.145 --> 00:13:07.830
their limits, and the
decisions the HR track needs.

201
00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:15.317
Stage four replaces walking the
tree with evaluating a rule.

202
00:13:15.317 --> 00:13:18.352
Instead of asking who sits
under the Finance branch,

203
00:13:18.352 --> 00:13:20.468
you ask who carries the Finance code.

204
00:13:20.468 --> 00:13:25.373
Every governed group uses one of two
patterns: a node query on one organization

205
00:13:25.373 --> 00:13:30.656
code, or a functional query that crosses
organization with something like position.

206
00:13:30.656 --> 00:13:36.180
And every access rule explicitly leaves
out disabled accounts, guests and agents.

207
00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:39.063
Convert only the groups
whose membership really

208
00:13:39.063 --> 00:13:41.805
follows the organization,
and flatten nesting.

209
00:13:41.805 --> 00:13:45.676
The gate is strict: validate,
shadow against the old group,

210
00:13:45.676 --> 00:13:49.217
and pilot before any rule
grants production access.

211
00:13:49.217 --> 00:13:51.980
Next, who gets to manage these people.

212
00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:58.977
Stage five moves delegation,
and it matters because this

213
00:13:58.977 --> 00:14:02.155
is where help desks and
administrators get their reach.

214
00:14:02.155 --> 00:14:06.266
In Entra ID, a role is assigned
over an administrative unit,

215
00:14:06.266 --> 00:14:09.093
so it applies only to that unit's members.

216
00:14:09.093 --> 00:14:12.973
The figure is the three-tier
model: readers across the tenant,

217
00:14:12.973 --> 00:14:15.767
then department units,
then division units.

218
00:14:15.767 --> 00:14:18.911
Decide restriction for each
unit before it exists,

219
00:14:18.911 --> 00:14:22.129
with a default of no, because
you can't change it later.

220
00:14:22.129 --> 00:14:23.517
And remember the callout.

221
00:14:23.517 --> 00:14:26.992
A unit limits who can change
things, not who can see them.

222
00:14:26.992 --> 00:14:28.570
Then, policy.

223
00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:35.132
Stage six retires Group
Policy inheritance,

224
00:14:35.132 --> 00:14:39.083
and the risk is policies that
look strict but restrict nothing.

225
00:14:39.083 --> 00:14:42.948
Conditional Access targets
personas built from attributes;

226
00:14:42.948 --> 00:14:47.344
employees and contractors, for example,
come from the worker type code.

227
00:14:47.344 --> 00:14:51.454
The figure is the key pattern: a
grant policy on its own doesn't

228
00:14:51.454 --> 00:14:55.645
stop anyone outside the group, so
pair every grant with a block.

229
00:14:55.645 --> 00:14:59.807
Run everything in report-only
first, then enforce in waves.

230
00:14:59.807 --> 00:15:05.571
And watch for empty attributes, because a
dynamic group silently misses those users.

231
00:15:05.571 --> 00:15:09.083
Devices are the other half,
and Part 2 takes them up.

232
00:15:09.083 --> 00:15:12.390
First, the dangers of dynamic user groups.

233
00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.628
Dynamic groups take away the ticket queue,

234
00:15:18.628 --> 00:15:22.040
and with it the human check, so
it's worth naming the dangers.

235
00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:25.621
Whoever can write an attribute
controls the access it grants,

236
00:15:25.621 --> 00:15:28.290
so only the provisioning
service writes them.

237
00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:31.343
A rule edit can remove
hundreds of people at once,

238
00:15:31.343 --> 00:15:34.702
so validate, shadow and pilot
it under change control.

239
00:15:34.702 --> 00:15:38.362
Rules need guards against
disabled accounts and guests,

240
00:15:38.362 --> 00:15:41.553
codes instead of names, and
a plan for empty values.

241
00:15:41.553 --> 00:15:46.927
Removal is slow and doesn't revoke tokens,
so urgent cases disable and revoke.

242
00:15:46.927 --> 00:15:48.952
And watch the membership deltas.

243
00:15:48.952 --> 00:15:50.880
Then, the limits.

244
00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:57.955
Every design has edges, and
these are the ones that bite.

245
00:15:57.955 --> 00:16:00.240
Privileged groups can't be dynamic,

246
00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:05.205
so privilege stays assigned and time-bound,
and HR decides only eligibility.

247
00:16:05.205 --> 00:16:09.567
Rules have size and count limits, and
they can't read some native fields.

248
00:16:09.567 --> 00:16:13.738
Then the date: the member-of
preview ends on November 3,

249
00:16:13.738 --> 00:16:17.055
2026, and groups that
use it freeze in place.

250
00:16:17.055 --> 00:16:19.720
And membership can lag by more than a day,

251
00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:23.355
so an urgent removal means
disable and revoke, not wait.

252
00:16:23.355 --> 00:16:26.320
So, the decisions for the HR track.

253
00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:35.295
Every decision here has one owner, and that's
what keeps the HR track from stalling.

254
00:16:35.295 --> 00:16:39.245
Identity engineering owns the
plumbing and the delegation model.

255
00:16:39.245 --> 00:16:43.570
The ICAM governance body owns the
authority map and the person key.

256
00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:46.838
HR owns the codes, the
data architect the schema,

257
00:16:46.838 --> 00:16:50.107
and application owners
their own access rules.

258
00:16:50.107 --> 00:16:52.207
The CISO owns the controls.

259
00:16:52.207 --> 00:16:57.675
And you can start Monday without new tools:
export the tree, name the HR feed early,

260
00:16:57.675 --> 00:17:02.396
populate the person key, remove other
writers, and route your logs out.

261
00:17:02.396 --> 00:17:03.820
So what's left?

262
00:17:07.570 --> 00:17:09.782
That's the model and the HR track.

263
00:17:09.782 --> 00:17:13.845
Part 2 takes the other three
tracks, and then runs the migration.

264
00:17:13.845 --> 00:17:18.748
First the Intune and Arc tracks: what
each tool covers, how devices and servers

265
00:17:18.748 --> 00:17:23.345
get their attributes, and what to do
with the devices neither one reaches.

266
00:17:23.345 --> 00:17:28.666
Then the non-person track: service
accounts, workload identities and AI agents,

267
00:17:28.666 --> 00:17:31.695
each with an owner and
a credential that ends.

268
00:17:31.695 --> 00:17:36.732
And finally the cutover, drift, the
limits and the decisions for every track.

269
00:17:36.732 --> 00:17:41.310
Part 2 covers devices, servers
and non-person identities.
